---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Playground from '../components/demos/Playground.astro';
import Bench from '../components/demos/Bench.astro';
import Diagnostics from '../components/demos/Diagnostics.astro';
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Live demos',
    description:
      'The engine, running in your browser: a book you can edit, a bench you can run, and the diagnostics channel with something wrong in it.',
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    fleuron makes two claims: that a book paginates in hundreds of
    milliseconds, and that the preview is exactly the export. Both are
    checkable on your own machine, so they are checked here rather than
    quoted from someone else's laptop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything below runs the real engine, compiled to WebAssembly, in a
    worker. The worker holds the module and has never seen an element; the
    page holds the DOM and has never seen the engine. What crosses between
    them is a display list, and the SVG on screen is painted from it.
  </p>

  <h2>The playground</h2>

  <p>
    A chapter of <em>Pride and Prejudice</em> and the stylesheet that sets it.
    Change either and the book is laid out again, a fifth of a second after you
    stop typing. The stylesheet editor can be closed from the settings; what
    you end up with is in the URL, so a question about the CSS subset can be a
    link.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Playground demo="playground" />
  </div>

  <h2>The bench</h2>

  <p>
    <em>Pride and Prejudice</em> entire, fetched and laid out here. Each row
    says which stages the engine ran for it, taken from its own counters, so a
    row that reused a stage cannot pass itself off as a row that ran one. The
    machine is named because a number without one is marketing.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Bench />
  </div>

  <h2>Diagnostics</h2>

  <p>
    A declaration outside <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}css-subset/`}
      >the subset</a
    > is reported at the line and column it was written at, and the book lays out
    anyway. Take one out, or put a new one in, and watch the report change.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-frame">
    <Diagnostics />
  </div>

  <h2>What this costs</h2>

  <p>
    The module weighs about 3.7&nbsp;MB over the wire, because it carries its
    own text face, its own hyphenation patterns and its own segmentation data:
    the engine reads no files and asks its host for nothing. Nothing on this
    page fetches it until a demo scrolls into view, no other page on this site
    fetches it at all, and a browser that says the connection is metered is
    asked before anything is spent.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style is:global>
  .demo-frame {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
